   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Research Question"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Does seasonal temperature varibiability, which will be measured by the standard deviation of average monthly temperatures, help explain differences in location-based happiness averages, measured by two studies done by the University of Pennsylvania?\n",
    "Our research will attempt to minimize the influence of confounding variables by accounting for wealth disparities between differernt regions.\n",
    "\n",
    "##### **Subquestions:** \n",
    "- Do the top 10 happiest cities experience lower seasonal teperature variability than the bottom 10 least happy cities?\n",
    "- How does the relationship between temperature variability and happiness differ in citites with higher vs. lower percentages of high-income households?\n",
    "- Which factor more closely aligns with happiness levels: average temperature or temperature variability\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Background and Prior Work"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The relationship between weather and human well-being has been a topic of interest in environmental research for years, with strong connections to psychological studies. Numerous studies have shown that weather conditions such as sunny days tend to improve mood, while rainy days can increase feelings of sadness. Although these findings are valuable, weather is a complex phenomenon that encompasses a wide range of factors, making it challenging to grasp its direct effects on human well-being. To address this, our research aims to investigate the role of temperature variability within a single day. We will examine whether the fluctuation between daytime high and nighttime low temperatures has a profound effect on a city’s overall happiness. By focusing on temperature variability rather than climate trends, we can more accurately identify patterns that reveal how weather affects happiness levels.\n",
    "\n",
    "Past research has utilized long-term average temperatures to draw conclusions about the impact of weather on happiness. For example, Rehdanz’s study of climate trends in 67 countries, using mean temperature and precipitation in the hottest and coldest months, found that higher temperatures during the coldest month were associated with greater happiness.<a name=\"cite_ref-1\"></a>[<sup>1</sup>](#cite_note-1) Another study by Tsutsui found that happiness was highest at a maximum temperature of 13.9 Celsius.<a name=\"cite_ref-2\"></a>[<sup>2</sup>](#cite_note-2) However, the results also indicated that although temperature influences people’s happiness, there is considerable variation in their responses. Few studies have explored how day-to-day weather changes, such as shifts between extreme and low temperatures, affect individuals’ happiness. A notable exception is research by Lucas and Lawless, which analyzed data from 1 million Americans over five years, combining different daily weather variables with life satisfaction ratings. They concluded that there was no significant correlation between temperature and life satisfaction judgments.<a name=\"cite_ref-3\"></a>[<sup>3</sup>](#cite_note-3)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Our work narrows the focus to temperature variability and its relationship with happiness at the city level. Past work has tendened to focus on average temperatures and its relationship to happiness. Does the overall consistency of the weather, consistently hot or cold rather than prone to large fluctutions throughout the year, correlate with overall happiness. Does stability bring contentment and peace?**\n",
    "\n",
    "This question connects to a broader idea in psychology: the more uncontrolled factors in your life, the more likely they are to impact your mental health. Weather, as an external and often unpredictable force, may function as one of these factors. If we view stability as a source of comfort and predictability, then cities with more stable weather patterns might foster a greater sense of peace and happiness among residents. Constant change, even in something as mundane as temperature, could contribute to stress or discomfort. Does greater stability in the environment support mental well-being? This is the central question our project seeks to explore.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. <a name=\"cite_note-1\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-1) Rehdanz, Katrin (8 Dec 2004) Climate and Happiness. *Ecological Economics*. https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800904002940 \n",
    "2. <a name=\"cite_note-2\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-2) Tsutsui, Yoshiro (1 Jan 2013) Weather and Individual Happiness. *Weather, Climate, and Society*. https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/wcas/5/1/wcas-d-11-00052_1.xml \n",
    "3. <a name=\"cite_note-3\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-3) Lucas, Richard E. and Lawless, Nicole M. (1 May 2014) Does Life Seem Better on a Sunny Day? *National Library of Medicine*. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3695376/ \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Hypothesis\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Our hypothesis is that there is a negative correlation between average happiness levels in a geographical area and the standard deviation from its mean temperature. That is to say that more consistent weather temperatures promotes a generally happier population.\n",
    "\n",
    "The thought process here centers around stability. The more stability and dependability there is in your life, the more that you can control the outcome of your day and lifetime. Thus consistency in the weather provides a dependable variable in your routine that will lead to increased happiness.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
