   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data overview\n",
    "\n",
    "For each dataset include the following information\n",
    "- Dataset #1\n",
    "  - Dataset Name: **Where Americans Are Happiest – 2024 Study**\n",
    "  - Link to the dataset: https://smartasset.com/data-studies/where-americans-are-happiest-2024\n",
    "  - Number of observations: 20\n",
    "  - Number of variables: 12\n",
    "  - Description: Each Happiness dataset lists the top 50 most “happy” cities in the US (the 2024 dataset also lists the top 10 least “happy” cities and lists the top 90 most “happy” cities). These datasets are more limited compared to the climate datasets in both time span and scope. They only list a happiness score which is a rather subjective measure but they are still solid datasets overall that list additional possibly confounding variables such as income level, life expectancy, and marriage rate. \n",
    "- Dataset #2\n",
    "  - Dataset Name: **Where Americans Are Happiest – 2022 Study**\n",
    "  - Link to the dataset: https://smartasset.com/data-studies/where-americans-are-happiest-2022\n",
    "  - Number of observations: 50\n",
    "  - Number of variables: 5\n",
    "  - Description: The 2022 SmartAsset dataset ranks the top 50 happiest cities in the U.S. based on a composite happiness score. Each city’s overall score is derived from three main components: Personal Finance Score, Well-Being Score, and Quality of Life Score. This dataset is more limited in scope than the 2024 version, with fewer variables and no data for lower-ranked or unhappy cities. However, it still provides a valuable snapshot of how financial stability, lifestyle, and public infrastructure contributed to happiness scores in 2022. Because the scoring is normalized, the dataset allows for direct comparison across cities but may lack the raw details needed to explain why those scores differ.\n",
    "- Dataset #3\n",
    "  - Dataset Name: **Climate Data Online Search**\n",
    "  - Link to the dataset: https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/cdo-web/search?datasetid=GHCND\n",
    "  - Number of observations: 120\n",
    "  - Number of variables: 8\n",
    "  - Description: The Climate Online Search data set provides monthly climate data across U.S. cities, including the mean, min, max temperature, precipitation, extreme values, and other measurements. City names will be matched to those in the happiness dataset. We are filtering out the relevant cities and their monthly ranges, converting their units in order to calculate monthly averages and totals respectively.\n",
    "\n",
    "Now write 2 - 5 sentences describing each dataset here. Include a short description of the important variables in the dataset; what the metrics and datatypes are, what concepts they may be proxies for. Include information about how you would need to wrangle/clean/preprocess the dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "If you plan to use multiple datasets, add a few sentences about how you plan to combine these datasets."
   ]
  },
